WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:10.400
If your organization has determined that&nbsp;
data governance will play an important role,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:13.760
you'll need to know who will be impacted&nbsp;
and what these people will need to do.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:18.640
Data governance doesn't necessarily&nbsp;
have to incur a lot of overhead cost.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:24.080
Generally, a big investment isn't required&nbsp;
in a whole bunch of new software tools.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:29.120
Where most overhead is felt is with the&nbsp;
introduction of new responsibilities for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:34.880
individuals. In large organizations this may&nbsp;
include hiring more and more dedicated staff.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:40.000
This is not unusual, as governance,&nbsp;
risk, and compliance requirements of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:44.400
many industries require specialized skills&nbsp;
and onerous reporting of deliverables.

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:49.280
Let's talk about one of the most common&nbsp;
mistakes organizations make with data.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:55.680
It seems quite normal that we assign people to own&nbsp;
processes such as the Human Resources Director,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.680 --> 00:01:00.960
who's accountable for recruitment, or a Facilities&nbsp;
Manager, who is responsible for real estate needs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:05.520
But, when it comes to data, we often&nbsp;
don't assign owners to that data.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:12.000
Organizations omit to give responsibility for&nbsp;
the maintenance of management of the data. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:16.720
seems intuitive that the Chief Financial Officer&nbsp;
should have responsibility for protecting credit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:22.480
card information. Or the Sales Director should&nbsp;
ultimately own accurate monthly sales reports.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:30.080
Let's be clear. Owning the data doesn't mean it&nbsp;
belongs to somebody. Ownership means being held&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:36.080
accountable for such things as data integrity,&nbsp;
security, and compliance requirements. We want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:41.360
owners to really care about the data with which&nbsp;
they have been entrusted. Since data governance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:46.560
is an enterprise concern, these data owners are&nbsp;
ultimately accountable to an authority that has an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:52.560
enterprise perspective. With data flowing between&nbsp;
systems internally and externally, a single&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:58.560
data owner has insufficient visibility for an&nbsp;
enterprise role. For this, we need data stewards.

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:05.040
A data steward can be a single individual&nbsp;
or a team of people. The data steward is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:11.200
the enterprise authority. It's the individual or&nbsp;
team ultimately responsible for data governance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:17.120
They create the rules and processes for governing&nbsp;
data and anticipate that data owners will follow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:23.120
those rules and processes. Who are these&nbsp;
data stewards typically? They are existing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:28.240
members of your organization. They might be&nbsp;
people, who, by the nature of their skills,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:34.160
are well-suited to participating in a data&nbsp;
oversight capacity. Data stewards usually conduct&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.160 --> 00:02:38.880
this role for just a few hours a month and spend&nbsp;
the rest of their time in their normal day jobs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:43.280
The data steward function is often&nbsp;
called the Data Governance Board,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:49.440
the Data Stewardship Council, or the Data&nbsp;
Governance Office. In medium to large enterprises,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:54.080
these groups are made up of several data stewards&nbsp;
that represent the entirety of the organization.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.040 --> 00:02:59.600
To maintain independence, data stewards are&nbsp;
typically different people than data owners.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:06.320
The group's activities include agreeing on data&nbsp;
policy and procedures, resolving data challenges,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.320 --> 00:03:10.640
reviewing concerns, and making strategic&nbsp;
decisions regarding the good management&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:16.800
of data in the enterprise. Data stewards use&nbsp;
information provided by data owners and managers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:22.080
and a wide range of stakeholders in order&nbsp;
to diligently conduct their work. The scope&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:27.200
of data owners and data stewards is an important&nbsp;
organizational decision and should be determined&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:39.840
in each organization based on such dependencies&nbsp;
as maturity, scale, and industry requirements.

